# Line endings
#
# Without this file, the checkout line ending is decided by each contributor's
# local core.autocrlf, so two people can have different bytes for the same
# tracked file. That is not theoretical here: with core.autocrlf=true on
# Windows, 182 of the tracked files landed in the working tree as CRLF while
# Prettier (which defaults to endOfLine: "lf") still expects LF, so
# `npm run format:check` failed on 150 files before this file existed.
#
# Pin it in the repo instead: store LF, check out LF, on every platform.
# Prettier's default and this file now agree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts are the exception - they need CRLF to run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Interpreted scripts run on Linux and in CI. CRLF in a shell script or a
# Python file produces a confusing error, so these stay LF.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Generated. npm writes LF; .prettierignore already excludes it from
# formatting, so this just stops anything else rewriting it.
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Container and CI entrypoints are read by other runtimes, never a Windows
# shell. Keep them LF so the image does not change between platforms.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Binary assets. `text=auto` already detects these, but naming them keeps a
# stray conversion from corrupting them if detection ever guesses wrong.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary